  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 21, $j$6.1 Exponential Growth and Decay$j$, $j${"body": "An exponential function has the form y = a·bˣ, where a is the starting value and b is the growth factor. If b > 1 it is growth. If 0 < b < 1 it is decay. A rate r gives b = 1 + r for growth and b = 1 − r for decay.\n\nLinear versus exponential: a linear function adds the same amount each step (constant difference). An exponential function multiplies by the same factor each step (constant ratio).\n\nCompound interest, yearly: A = P(1 + r)ᵗ. $1,000 at 5% for 2 years: 1000(1.05)² = $1,102.50. For 3 years: $1,157.63. Simple interest for 3 years would give only $150, but compounding gives $157.63.\nDecay: a car worth $20,000 loses 15% each year, so V = 20000(0.85)ᵗ. After 2 years: 20000(0.7225) = $14,450.\nDoubling: P = 100·2ᵗ gives 3,200 after 5 doubling periods.\n\nThink it through: exponential change multiplies, and that is why it grows so fast. In 1000(1.05)², you multiply by 1.05 twice, which is not the same as 1000 · 1.05 · 2. To build a growth factor from a rate, add the rate to 1, so 7 percent growth uses 1.07. For decay, subtract it from 1, so 7 percent decay uses 0.93. The parable of the talents in Matthew 25, verses 14 to 30, teaches faithful use of what has been entrusted to us. Understanding how money grows over time is part of wise stewardship, and compound interest is a tool that rewards patience and discipline.\n\nYour task: (a) Evaluate 500(1.1)³. (b) Evaluate 80(0.5)³. (c) Is y = 3(1.2)ˣ growth or decay? Is y = 50(0.9)ˣ? (d) Bacteria triples each hour, starting at 4. How many are there after 3 hours?\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.AR.5.3, MA.912.AR.5.4, MA.912.AR.5.6, MA.912.FL.3.2, MA.912.FL.3.4.", "video": "", "script": "Welcome to a topic that explains how money grows, how populations spread, and how things lose value. An exponential function is a function in which the variable is in the exponent. We write it as y equals a times b to the x. The number a is the starting value, and b is the growth factor, the number we multiply by each step.\n\nFirst, compare it with a line. A linear function adds the same amount each time. A table with outputs two, five, eight, eleven is linear, because we add three. An exponential function multiplies by the same factor each time. Outputs two, six, eighteen, fifty four are exponential, because we multiply by three each time.\n\nIf the factor b is greater than one, the quantity grows. If b is between zero and one, the quantity decays. We can build b from a percent rate. For a growth of five percent, b equals one plus zero point zero five, or one point oh five. For a loss of fifteen percent, b equals one minus zero point fifteen, or zero point eighty five.\n\nLet us try compound interest. You invest one thousand dollars at five percent each year. After one year you have one thousand fifty. After two years, we multiply one thousand by one point oh five squared, which is one thousand one hundred two dollars and fifty cents. After three years it is one thousand one hundred fifty seven dollars and sixty three cents. With simple interest, you would earn only fifty dollars a year, one hundred fifty in three years. Compounding earned you seven dollars and sixty three cents more, because you earned interest on your interest. Give it thirty years, and the difference becomes large.\n\nDecay works the same way. A car worth twenty thousand dollars loses fifteen percent of its value each year. After two years, the value is twenty thousand times zero point eighty five squared. That is twenty thousand times zero point seven two two five, or fourteen thousand four hundred fifty dollars.\n\nOne more. A culture of bacteria starts at one hundred and doubles every period. After five periods, it is one hundred times two to the fifth, which is three thousand two hundred.\n\nYour task today is to evaluate four exponential expressions and decide between growth and decay. Then take the four question check.\n\nScripture warns that the borrower is servant to the lender, and teaches that he that gathereth by labour shall increase. Compounding rewards patient, steady saving and punishes debt. Use it wisely. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.AR.5.3, MA.912.AR.5.4, MA.912.AR.5.6, MA.912.FL.3.2, MA.912.FL.3.4"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.1 Exponential Growth and Decay$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 22, $j$6.2 Comparing Models and Transformations$j$, $j${"body": "Comparing growth: a linear function eventually grows more slowly than an exponential function with b > 1, even if the line starts higher.\nExample: f(x) = 100x and g(x) = 2ˣ. At x = 9, f = 900 and g = 512. At x = 10, f = 1000 and g = 1024. From x = 10 on, the exponential is larger.\n\nTransformations of a parent function f(x):\nf(x) + k shifts the graph up k (down if k is negative).\nf(x + h) shifts the graph left h (right if h is negative).\n−f(x) reflects the graph over the x axis.\nk·f(x) with k > 1 stretches it vertically; with 0 < k < 1 it compresses it.\n\nWith f(x) = x²: x² + 3 is up 3. (x − 2)² is right 2. −x² opens down. 2x² is narrower. For g(x) = (x + 4)² − 1: left 4, down 1, vertex (−4, −1).\n\nThink it through: transformations move a graph without changing its basic shape. The rule that surprises most students is the horizontal shift. In (x + 4)², the plus sign moves the graph left, not right, because the vertex occurs where x + 4 = 0, which is x = −4. Vertical changes behave as you expect: adding 3 lifts the graph 3 units. Comparing models, a line may start far ahead, but an exponential with a base greater than 1 will eventually pass it. When you compare 100x and 2ˣ, make a table of values for x = 1 through 12 and look for where the exponential first becomes larger.\n\nYour task: (a) Describe g(x) = (x + 4)² − 1. (b) What does −f(x) do to a graph? (c) Find the first whole number x greater than 0 where 2ˣ is larger than 100x.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.F.1.6, MA.912.F.1.8, MA.912.F.2.1.", "video": "", "script": "Today we compare different kinds of growth and learn how to move graphs around without starting over each time.\n\nFirst, comparing models. Take a line, f of x equals one hundred x, and an exponential, g of x equals two to the x. At first the line looks far ahead. At x equals nine, f is nine hundred and g is five hundred twelve. The line is still ahead. But at x equals ten, f is one thousand and g is one thousand twenty four. The exponential has taken the lead, and it will never give it back. This is the lesson: no matter how large the line's slope, an exponential with a factor greater than one will eventually win. That is why a small amount of steady compounding can beat a large single deposit over a long time.\n\nNow transformations. A parent function is the simplest version of a family, like y equals x squared. We can change it in predictable ways.\n\nAdding a number outside the function moves the graph up or down. x squared plus three is the parent shifted up three.\n\nAdding a number inside the parentheses moves the graph left or right, and the direction feels backward. The graph of x minus two, squared, shifts right two, not left. The graph of x plus four, squared, shifts left four.\n\nPutting a negative sign in front of the whole function flips the graph over the x axis, so a parabola that opened up now opens down.\n\nMultiplying the function by a number larger than one stretches the graph vertically, making a narrower parabola. Two x squared is narrower than x squared. Multiplying by a number between zero and one makes it wider.\n\nCombine them. For g of x equals x plus four, squared, minus one, we start from x squared, shift left four and down one. The vertex moves from zero comma zero to negative four comma negative one. You can read the whole story from the equation.\n\nThe same ideas work for exponentials. Three times two to the x has a y intercept of three, because the starting value is three.\n\nYour task today is to describe a transformation, to explain what a negative sign does, and to find where an exponential first beats a line. Then take the four question check.\n\nJob said, Though thy beginning was small, yet thy latter end should greatly increase. In mathematics and in faith, the slow starter with steady growth can win the long race. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.F.1.6, MA.912.F.1.8, MA.912.F.2.1"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.2 Comparing Models and Transformations$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 23, $j$6.3 Data Displays and Distributions$j$, $j${"body": "Choose a display to fit the question: a dot plot for small sets, a histogram for grouped numerical data (bar heights show frequency), and a box plot to show the five number summary (minimum, Q1, median, Q3, maximum).\n\nMeasures of center: mean (the sum divided by the count) and median (the middle value).\nMeasures of spread: range (maximum minus minimum) and interquartile range IQR = Q3 − Q1.\n\nData: 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12. Mean = 60/8 = 7.5. Median = (7 + 8)/2 = 7.5. Q1 = 5.5, Q3 = 9.5, IQR = 4. Range = 9.\nAdd the value 30: 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12, 30. The mean becomes 90/9 = 10, but the median is 8. An outlier pulls the mean much more than the median. Test: values beyond Q3 + 1.5·IQR (here 11 + 8.25 = 19.25) are outliers, so 30 is one.\nFor skewed data or data with outliers, the median and IQR describe the data better than the mean.\n\nThink it through: no single number describes a data set perfectly, so choose the one that fits. The mean uses every value, so a very large or very small value pulls it. The median is the middle value, so it stays steady. News reports on household income usually use the median, because a few extremely high incomes would pull the mean upward and give a false picture of a typical family. Use the IQR to describe spread when you use the median. A box plot shows the five number summary at a glance: the box holds the middle half of the data, and a long whisker shows a stretched out tail.\n\nYour task: for 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14 find the mean, median, range, Q1, Q3 and IQR. Then tell which measure of center you would report for a data set with a very large outlier, and why.\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.DP.1.1, MA.912.DP.1.2, MA.912.DP.1.4, MA.912.DP.3.1.", "video": "", "script": "Data tells a story, and a good display helps us read it. Today we learn to describe a set of numbers, to choose a display, and to decide which summary is honest.\n\nLet us start with displays. A dot plot places a dot above a number line for each value. It is perfect for small sets. A histogram groups values into intervals, with the height of each bar showing how many values fall in that interval. It is best for large sets. A box plot shows five numbers: the minimum, the first quartile, the median, the third quartile and the maximum. The box shows the middle half of the data.\n\nNow measures of center. The mean is the sum of all values divided by how many there are. The median is the middle value when the numbers are in order. If there are two middle values, the median is their average.\n\nMeasures of spread tell how far the numbers range. The range is the maximum minus the minimum. The interquartile range, or IQR, is the third quartile minus the first quartile, the width of the middle half.\n\nHere is a data set: three, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten, twelve. The sum is sixty, and there are eight values, so the mean is seven point five. The two middle values are seven and eight, so the median is also seven point five. The first quartile is the median of the lower half, which is five and a half. The third quartile is nine and a half. The IQR is four, and the range is nine.\n\nNow watch what happens when we add one very large value, thirty. The sum is ninety, nine values, so the mean jumps to ten. But the median, the middle of nine values, is only eight. The mean was pulled far by one value, and the median barely moved. To test for an outlier, we compute the third quartile plus one and a half times the IQR. Here, that is eleven plus eight and a quarter, or nineteen and a quarter. Thirty is beyond that, so it is an outlier.\n\nThis is why we say that for skewed data or data with outliers, the median and the IQR are the better choice. They resist being dragged by extreme values. For roughly symmetric data, the mean works well.\n\nYour task today is to compute the summary for a small set and to explain your choice of measure. Then take the four question check.\n\nThe Psalmist prayed, So teach us to number our days, that we may apply our hearts unto wisdom. Counting well, and describing honestly, is part of wisdom. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.DP.1.1, MA.912.DP.1.2, MA.912.DP.1.4, MA.912.DP.3.1"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.3 Data Displays and Distributions$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 24, $j$6.4 Scatter Plots, Correlation and Lines of Best Fit$j$, $j${"body": "A scatter plot shows two quantities as points. The pattern shows the correlation: positive (as x rises, y rises), negative (as x rises, y falls) or none. The correlation coefficient r runs from −1 to 1; values near 1 or −1 are strong, values near 0 are weak. Correlation does not prove causation.\n\nData: hours studied x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and test scores y = 62, 70, 74, 82, 88. The line of best fit is y = 6.4x + 56, with r about 0.996, a strong positive correlation.\nSlope 6.4: each extra hour of study goes with about 6.4 more points. Intercept 56: the predicted score at zero hours.\nPrediction (interpolation, inside the data): x = 6 gives 6.4(6) + 56 = 94.4. A residual is actual minus predicted: at x = 2, 70 − 68.8 = 1.2.\nBe careful with extrapolation (predicting far outside the data): x = 20 would predict 184, which is impossible for a test score.\n\nThink it through: a strong correlation means two quantities move together. It does not mean one causes the other. Ice cream sales and sunburns both rise in summer, but ice cream does not cause sunburn. The warm weather is the hidden cause. A line of best fit lets you make predictions inside your data, called interpolation, with reasonable confidence. Predictions far outside the data, called extrapolation, can fail badly, as the 184 point test score showed. A residual measures how far a real point sits from the line, so small residuals mean the line fits well. Always explain slope and intercept in the words of the story.\n\nYour task: (a) Predict the score for 4 hours. (b) Interpret the slope. (c) Why is the prediction at 20 hours unreliable? (d) What does r = −0.9 mean?\n\nFlorida standards: MA.912.DP.1.3, MA.912.DP.2.4, MA.912.DP.2.6.", "video": "", "script": "When two things seem to move together, we ask a question: is there a relationship? A scatter plot is how we look. Each point shows two measurements for one individual, such as hours studied and test score.\n\nThe pattern we see is called correlation. If the points rise from left to right, the correlation is positive. If they fall, it is negative. If they look like a cloud with no direction, there is no correlation. The correlation coefficient, called r, measures strength. It runs from negative one to positive one. A value near one or negative one is strong. A value near zero is weak.\n\nOne warning must always travel with correlation: correlation does not prove causation. Ice cream sales and sunburns rise together, but ice cream does not cause sunburn. Heat and sun are behind both. Before saying that one thing causes another, we need more than a pattern.\n\nNow a line of best fit. Suppose five students studied one, two, three, four and five hours, and scored sixty two, seventy, seventy four, eighty two and eighty eight. A calculator or software finds the best line, y equals six point four x plus fifty six. The correlation is about zero point nine nine six, very strong and positive.\n\nLet us interpret the line. The slope, six point four, means that each extra hour of study goes with about six point four more points. The intercept, fifty six, is the predicted score with zero hours. \n\nWe can predict. For four hours, six point four times four is twenty five point six, plus fifty six is eighty one point six. For six hours, we get ninety four point four. Predicting inside the range of the data is called interpolation, and it is fairly reliable. We can also compute a residual, which is the actual value minus the predicted value. For two hours, the line predicts sixty eight point eight, and the actual score was seventy, so the residual is positive one point two.\n\nBut watch out for extrapolation, predicting far outside the data. At twenty hours, the line says one hundred eighty four, which is impossible for a test score. The pattern holds only within a reasonable range.\n\nYour task today is to predict, interpret the slope, explain why extrapolation can mislead, and read the meaning of a correlation. Then take the four question check.\n\nRemember, Prove all things; hold fast that which is good. Test your conclusions before you trust them. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson.", "readings": [], "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "MA.912.DP.1.3, MA.912.DP.2.4, MA.912.DP.2.6"}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$6.4 Scatter Plots, Correlation and Lines of Best Fit$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'assessment', 6, $j$Assessment 6: Exponential Functions, Models and Data, Show Your Work$j$, $j${"prompt": "Show every step. (1) A savings account holds $2,000 and earns 4% interest compounded yearly. Write the model, find the balance after 3 years, and compare it with simple interest for 3 years. (2) A computer worth $1,200 loses 25% of its value each year. Write the model and find the value after 2 years. (3) Describe the transformation from f(x) = x² to g(x) = −(x − 3)² + 2, and state the vertex. (4) For the data 2, 4, 4, 6, 8, 10, 30, find the mean, the median, Q1, Q3 and IQR, decide whether 30 is an outlier, and say which measure of center you would report. (5) A line of best fit is y = 3x + 10 for x, hours of practice per week, and y, number of free throws made out of 50. Predict y for x = 5, interpret the slope, and explain why x = 30 is unreliable.", "rubric": "Total 100. Item 1: 22 (A = 2000(1.04)ᵗ 6, balance $2,249.73 8, simple interest $240 giving $2,240 compared 8). Item 2: 16 (V = 1200(0.75)ᵗ 8, $675 8). Item 3: 16 (reflected over the x axis 4, right 3 4, up 2 4, vertex (3, 2) 4). Item 4: 26 (mean 64/7 about 9.14 4, median 6 4, Q1 4 4, Q3 10 4, IQR 6 4, outlier test 19 so 30 is an outlier 4, median recommended 2). Item 5: 20 (25 6, slope meaning 6, extrapolation 8). Show of work counts in every item."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'assessment' and title = $j$Assessment 6: Exponential Functions, Models and Data, Show Your Work$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'discussion', 6, $j$Discussion 6: Numbers That Tell the Truth$j$, $j${"prompt": "Describe a statistic or a growth pattern you have seen used in a news story, a social media post or a ministry report. Was it presented honestly? Explain what extra information, such as an outlier, the median, or the difference between correlation and causation, would help you judge it. Respond in 100 to 150 words."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'discussion' and title = $j$Discussion 6: Numbers That Tell the Truth$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 26, $j$Check 6.1: Exponential Growth and Decay$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "You invest $1,000 at 5% interest compounded yearly. What is the value after 2 years?", "options": ["$1,050.00", "$1,102.50", "$1,200.00", "$1,100.00"]}, {"q": "In y = 200(0.8)ˣ, what does the 0.8 tell you?", "options": ["The quantity grows by 80% each step", "The quantity decays by 80% each step", "The quantity decays by 20% each step", "The starting value is 0.8"]}, {"q": "A population starts at 100 and doubles each period. What is it after 5 periods?", "options": ["1,000", "3,200", "10,000", "500"]}, {"q": "Which table of outputs is exponential?", "options": ["2, 4, 6, 8", "10, 8, 6, 4", "2, 6, 18, 54", "2, 5, 8, 11"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.1: Exponential Growth and Decay$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{1,2,1,2}'::int[], array[$j$1000(1.05)² = 1102.50.$j$,$j$b = 1 − 0.2, so the quantity loses 20% each step.$j$,$j$100·2⁵ = 3,200.$j$,$j$Each output is multiplied by 3.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.1: Exponential Growth and Decay$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 27, $j$Check 6.2: Comparing Models and Transformations$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "How does the graph of g(x) = x² + 5 compare with f(x) = x²?", "options": ["Shifted left 5", "Shifted right 5", "Shifted up 5", "Shifted down 5"]}, {"q": "The graph of (x − 3)² is the graph of x² shifted:", "options": ["Left 3", "Down 3", "Up 3", "Right 3"]}, {"q": "What does −f(x) do to the graph of f(x)?", "options": ["Moves it down 1", "Reflects it over the x axis", "Makes it wider", "Reflects it over the y axis"]}, {"q": "For f(x) = 100x and g(x) = 2ˣ, at which whole number x does g first exceed f?", "options": ["20", "9", "10", "5"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.2: Comparing Models and Transformations$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{2,3,1,2}'::int[], array[$j$Adding 5 outside the function moves it up.$j$,$j$Subtracting inside shifts the graph right.$j$,$j$A negative outside flips the graph over the x axis.$j$,$j$At x = 10, g = 1024 and f = 1000.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.2: Comparing Models and Transformations$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 28, $j$Check 6.3: Data Displays and Distributions$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "What is the mean of 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12?", "options": ["7.5", "7", "60", "8"]}, {"q": "What is the median of 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12, 30?", "options": ["7.5", "8", "10", "30"]}, {"q": "Which measure of center is best for a data set with a very large outlier?", "options": ["Mean", "Median", "Range", "Maximum"]}, {"q": "A histogram shows:", "options": ["The equation of the data", "Only the outliers", "How many values fall in each interval", "Only the median"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.3: Data Displays and Distributions$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{0,1,1,2}'::int[], array[$j$The sum is 60, and 60/8 = 7.5.$j$,$j$The middle (fifth) value is 8.$j$,$j$The median resists the pull of extreme values.$j$,$j$Bar heights show frequency for each interval.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.3: Data Displays and Distributions$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 29, $j$Check 6.4: Scatter Plots, Correlation and Lines of Best Fit$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "A line of best fit is y = 6.4x + 56 for hours studied (x) and score (y). What score does it predict for 6 hours?", "options": ["92", "38.4", "94.4", "62.4"]}, {"q": "In that line, what does the slope 6.4 mean?", "options": ["Each extra hour goes with about 6.4 more points", "The score with zero hours is 6.4", "The student studies 6.4 hours", "The correlation is 6.4"]}, {"q": "A scatter plot has points falling from left to right. The correlation is:", "options": ["Exponential", "Negative", "Positive", "Zero for certain"]}, {"q": "Two quantities have a strong correlation. Does this prove that one causes the other?", "options": ["Yes, if r is above 0.5", "Only if the line is steep", "No, correlation does not prove causation", "Yes, always"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.4: Scatter Plots, Correlation and Lines of Best Fit$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{2,0,1,2}'::int[], array[$j$6.4(6) + 56 = 94.4.$j$,$j$The slope is the rate of change of score per hour.$j$,$j$Falling points show a negative correlation.$j$,$j$A third factor or coincidence may explain the pattern.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Check 6.4: Scatter Plots, Correlation and Lines of Best Fit$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'quiz', 30, $j$Unit Quiz 6: Exponential Functions, Models and Data$j$, $j${"questions": [{"q": "What is 200(1.1)²?", "options": ["220", "400", "240", "242"]}, {"q": "Is y = 50(0.9)ˣ growth or decay?", "options": ["Linear", "Neither", "Decay", "Growth"]}, {"q": "What is the median of 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12, 30?", "options": ["8", "10", "30", "7"]}, {"q": "Which measure is least affected by an outlier?", "options": ["Sum", "Median", "Mean", "Range"]}, {"q": "A correlation coefficient of r = −0.95 shows:", "options": ["A strong negative correlation", "No correlation", "A weak positive correlation", "A strong positive correlation"]}, {"q": "The graph of g(x) = (x + 2)² compared with x² is shifted:", "options": ["Left 2", "Right 2", "Up 2", "Down 2"]}]}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 6: Exponential Functions, Models and Data$j$);
  insert into public.quiz_keys (item_id, answers, explains) select id, '{3,2,0,1,0,0}'::int[], array[$j$200 × 1.21 = 242.$j$,$j$The factor 0.9 is between 0 and 1.$j$,$j$The fifth value is 8.$j$,$j$The median resists extreme values.$j$,$j$Near −1 is strong and negative.$j$,$j$Adding inside shifts the graph left.$j$] from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'quiz' and title = $j$Unit Quiz 6: Exponential Functions, Models and Data$j$ on conflict (item_id) do nothing;
